13:25:01 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I get why it seems like that should be true, but it's actually a common misconception—your thermostat doesn't make every room the same temperature. Here's why: **The thermostat only measures air temperature at one spot** Your thermostat has a sensor in one location (usually a hallway or central spot). It calls for heat until *that specific spot* reads 72°F, then stops. Rooms farther from the furnace, rooms with more exterior walls, rooms above unheated garages, or rooms with poor airflow can easily be sitting at 65-68°F while the hallway sensor reads a happy 72°F. This is why upstairs bedrooms often run colder in winter, or why a room at the end of a duct run never quite catches up. **Even if the air were perfectly uniform, it still wouldn't feel uniform** This is the part that's not just "fancy physics"—it's basic comfort science HVAC engineers actually design around. Two rooms can both genuinely be 72°F on a thermometer and still feel different to your skin because: - One room has a big cold window radiating chill at you (radiant heat loss) - One room has a draft from an exterior door - One room has humidity sitting at 25% vs. 45% in another None of that requires the air temperature itself to be different. Your body doesn't have a thermometer—it has nerve endings that respond to actual heat loss, which depends on air temp *plus* radiant exchange, air movement, and humidity. So the short version: your house is almost certainly not uniformly 72°F right now even with one thermostat, and even in the room where it genuinely is 72°F, you can still feel colder in winter for the reasons above.
