13:25:50 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a really clever analogy, but it breaks down because water finding its level and rooms reaching the same temperature are actually different types of physics problems. **Water level equalizing is about pressure, not "mixing"** Water settles to the same level because gravity pulls on it uniformly and there's nothing counteracting that pull once it's flat. It's a *static* equilibrium—once it's level, nothing is happening anymore. No water is flowing anywhere. **Room temperature is a dynamic, ongoing battle** Heat isn't like that. Every room in your house is *continuously* losing heat to the outside—through windows, walls, roof, floors—and that loss rate is different for every room. A room with two exterior walls and a big window loses heat much faster than an interior room with no exterior walls. Meanwhile, your furnace is pumping heat back in, but not at an identical rate to every room either (duct length, vent size, how far from the furnace, whether doors are open). So it's not "will the air eventually settle to one level and stay there"—it's "can heat input keep pace with heat loss, in every room, equally, at every moment." That's a much harder condition to meet than gravity making water flat. **A closer analogy to your pipes idea:** Imagine pipes that are constantly springing tiny leaks at different rates along their length, while someone's pumping water back in at one end. The water level *would not* stay flat—it'd be lower wherever the leaks were worse, even though everything's "connected." That's closer to what's actually happening with heat in your house: constant uneven loss, constant refill from one source, and the system never quite catches up perfectly everywhere at once. That's why interior rooms typically run warmer than rooms with lots of exterior wall/window area, even on the same floor, on the same duct system, thermostat cranking away the whole time.
